# Feature Delivery Workflow

## Provenance
- Source: `ai-engineering/workflows/feature-development.md`
- Intent: enforce a predictable, architecture-safe implementation flow.

## Required Sequence
1. Analyze requirements and define acceptance criteria.
2. Analyze architecture boundaries and impacted layers.
3. Design solution and data flow before coding.
4. Implement domain/core logic first.
5. Implement application/use-case orchestration.
6. Implement infrastructure adapters/integrations.
7. Add tests (unit + integration + relevant end-to-end).
8. Review end-to-end behavior and regression risk.
9. Refine maintainability (naming, structure, dead code cleanup).

## Layering Rules
- Keep domain logic framework-agnostic.
- Outer layers may depend on inner layers, not vice versa.
- Use interfaces/contracts between layers.
- Do not move business logic into controllers/adapters.

## Decision Rules During Execution
- If request is ambiguous, ask one focused clarification before coding.
- If existing code conflicts with requested behavior, resolve explicitly (do not patch around it).
- If no tests exist around changed area, add baseline tests first when practical.
- If behavior might break existing flows, stop and flag risk clearly.
- If multiple valid options exist, choose simpler maintainable option and document tradeoff.

## Quality Gates Before Completion
- Requirements and acceptance criteria are fully covered.
- Error paths are handled explicitly.
- Naming is intention-revealing.
- No dead code/debug leftovers.
- Security and performance implications reviewed.
- Change summary includes what, why, how, impact, and tests.
